## Why This Is Asked

Tests system-level thinking for 3D reconstruction under dynamic scenes and edge-runtime constraints, plus evaluation rigor.

## Key Concepts

- Dense 3D representation (TSDF) with dynamic masking
- Lightweight pose estimation and masked fusion
- Dynamic voxel confidence and decay for occlusions
- Edge latency targets and robust evaluation metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode: update TSDF with dynamic masking
function updateVoxelTSDF(voxel, depth, pose, isDynamic) {
  if (isDynamic) return; // skip static updates for dynamic voxels until confirmed
  const sdf = projectDepthToVoxel(depth, pose, voxel.coord);
  voxel.tsdf = (voxel.tsdf * voxel.weight + sdf) / (voxel.weight + 1);
  voxel.weight += 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you integrate semantic priors to improve dynamic masking in cluttered warehouses?
- What ablation model would you run to measure the impact of dynamic masking on map quality and drift over 5 seconds?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates real-time monocular computer vision for avatar-driven video conferencing, with emphasis on three critical constraints: sub-30ms latency for natural interaction, on-device privacy for user trust, and rapid on-device adaptation for personalized gesture vocabularies. Success requires co-designing efficient models, privacy-preserving pipelines, and lightweight adaptation mechanisms—skills essential for building consumer-grade AR/VR experiences at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Monocular landmark detection, gesture recognition, and avatar rig mapping
- Edge latency budgets and resource-aware model architectures
- Few-shot on-device adaptation using synthetic and real data
- Privacy-by-design constraints and rigorous evaluation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton of the avatar estimator outline
class AvatarEstimator {
  constructor(model) {
    this.model = model;
    this.buffer = [];
  }

  infer(frame) {
    const landmarks = this.model.detect(frame);
    const avatarParams = this.mapToAvatar(landmarks);
    return avatarParams;
  }

  mapToAvatar(landmarks) {
    // Map 2D landmarks to 3D avatar rig parameters
    return {
      blendShapes: this.computeBlendShapes(landmarks),
      handPose: this.estimateHandPose(landmarks)
    };
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design cross-modal, real-time computer vision pipelines that integrate on-device adaptation with differentiable geometry for robotic manipulation and planning in unstructured environments.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time monocular depth and surface normals estimation from a single RGB camera
- Differentiable probabilistic mesh prediction integrated with motion planning
- Few-shot (5-shot) on-device adaptation using synthetic pre-training followed by real-world fine-tuning
- Multi-task learning with shared encoders and task-specific heads

## Code Example

```javascript
// Core inference pipeline sketch
function forwardPass(frame) {
  const features = sharedEncoder(frame);
  const depth = depthHead(features);
  const normals = normalHead(features);
  const mesh = meshHead(features);
  return { depth, normals, mesh };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you quantify uncertainty in the mesh predictions to ensure safe manipulation?
- What strategies would you use to handle reflective or texture-less surfaces where depth estimation typically fails?
- How does the system handle dynamic scene changes between adaptation and deployment?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes on-device real-time hazard detection and 3D distance estimation under unseen categories, reflecting safety-critical constraints common in field deployments.

## Key Concepts

- Real-time monocular depth on edge
- On-device meta-learning (5-shot)
- Synthetic+real data with domain randomization
- Temporal tracking and occlusion robustness
- Evaluation: detection, depth error, latency

## Code Example

```javascript
function forward(img, backbone, detectorHead, depthHead) {
  const feats = backbone.extract(img);
  const boxes = detectorHead.predict(feats);
  const depth = depthHead.predict(feats);
  return { boxes, depth };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you ablate depth head accuracy vs. detector latency?
- What failure modes dominate in cluttered sites, and how would you mitigate them?
